#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# sd-ai - the in-SD model helper of SD Core for Windows (from Linux b1d496df, S.69 step 2; RELEASE_1.1 246, design .claude/design-ai-helper-20261113.md).
#
# The Windows service sd-ai, running as the virtual account NT SERVICE\sd-ai, listens on the pipe \\.\pipe\sd-ai and starts ONE short-lived process of this
# program per connection (Linux: sd-ai.socket, Accept=true, one process per connection as the OS user sdai).  That process has the connection as its standard
# input and output: ONE JSON line in, JSON lines out.  Nothing else in SD connects outward; nothing else reads the keys.  Standard library only, under the
# all-users Python 3.13 or later the SD installer offers (the AI needs it, as Linux's needs python3).
#
#   python -I -B sd-ai --service                      the service itself (sd-ai's binPath; install-aihelper.ps1 registers it)
#   python -I -B sd-ai --one [--state DIR] [--providers FILE]       one request on standard input/output (what the service starts per connection)
#   python -I -B sd-ai --listen PIPE [--state DIR] [--providers FILE]   the service's loop in the foreground, on another pipe (the tests)
#
# REQUEST (one line, UTF-8 JSON):  {"v":1,"op":"ask","system":"...","prompt":"...","maxtokens":1024}      or  {"v":1,"op":"status"}
#                   an ask may also carry "history": [{"q":"...","a":"..."}, ...] (at most 3; a follow-up the person chose and saw in SD's preview)
# REPLY, one line:  {"t":"ok","text":"...","in":N,"out":N,"provider":"anthropic","truncated":false,"replaced":N}
#                   {"t":"err","code":"nogate|noadmin|disabled|nomodel|nokey|nolist|toobig|limit|net|timeout|refused|busy|declined|badreply|badreq|badconf","msg":"..."}
#                   {"t":"status", ...}      (status: provider, model, key set, gate; the connection details only for the administrator)
# THE SETTINGS are changed through the same pipe, by the administrator ONLY (the caller's token, read from the pipe, nothing the caller wrote):
#   {"v":1,"op":"set","what":"provider","value":"openai"}  {"what":"model","provider":"openai","value":"..."}  {"what":"custom","name":..,"url":..,"format":..,"auth":..,
#   "version":..,"maxparam":..}  {"what":"custom-clear"}  {"what":"ca","value":"C:/path"}  {"what":"limit","name":"tokens-day","value":"100000"}
#   {"v":1,"op":"keyset","provider":"openai","key":"..."}   {"v":1,"op":"keyclear","provider":"openai"}
#   {"v":1,"op":"models","provider":"deepseek"}  -> {"t":"models","provider":..,"models":["...", ...]}: the provider's own list of model names (8 Nov 26), the administrator only
#   {"what":"context","value":"..."} the standing text (S.160; "" puts the shipped default back).  status gives it to a gate member and the administrator as
#   "context" with "context-from" own | default | none | bad; SD sends it with every request and shows it in the preview.  The default is ai-context beside the
#   providers file (in the program folder, with this program).
# Every result is checked whole BEFORE it is written (one provider selected means its details are complete), the file is replaced atomically, and a key
# is never echoed or logged.  The administrator's own client is gplbld/sd-ai-ctl, which reads a key from the console and so keeps it out of SD.
#
# WHAT IT DECIDES, IN THIS ORDER (a caller learns nothing about the settings until the gate has let them through):
#   1 THE GATE: the caller's account, read from the pipe (ImpersonateNamedPipeClient at identification level; not from anything the caller wrote), must be a member
#     of the Windows group sdaiusers, read live at each request.  A caller that cannot be read, a service account (LocalSystem, LocalService, NetworkService), an
#     unreadable group or no such group all REFUSE: fail closed.  The administrator - the local account SDSYS, elevated - may ask without it (Linux S.163).
#   2 `disabled` when no provider is selected (the delivered state: no provider in ai.conf); `nomodel`; `nokey`.
#   3 `toobig` (the request), `limit` (requests per hour and tokens per day, per caller, in a locked file).
#   4 ONE HTTPS call to the URL of the selected provider: certificate verification mandatory (the Windows certificate store, or the ca-file setting for a
#     private CA), host name checked, TLS 1.2 or later, NO insecure mode and no flag that gives one, no redirect followed, the key sent only to
#     that URL, no proxy.  A reply is cut to ASCII plus newline and tab (a terminal escape sequence, an SD mark byte or a non-English letter never reaches
#     SD): typographic quotes and dashes become their plain forms, anything else becomes ?, and the number replaced is reported.
# NOTHING IS WRITTEN TO THE AUDIT TRAIL HERE: SD writes `ai request ...` BEFORE it asks, so a crash still leaves the record.  The one line this
# program writes per request, to its log (sd-ai.log in the state directory), has the caller's SID, the provider, the status and the sizes - never the text, never the key.
#
# WINDOWS FACTS (the Linux form is the template; each of these is forced by the operating system):
#   - the caller: Linux reads the uid off the socket (SO_PEERCRED); here the pipe gives the caller's token at identification level.  The gate group is read with
#     NetLocalGroupGetMembers, live, as Linux's getgrouplist is.
#   - the administrator: Linux's sdsys or root.  Here SDSYS (only ever an elevated sign-in) may ask and change the settings; another ELEVATED member of
#     Administrators may change the settings (owner, 13 Nov 2026, Q6) but asks only with the gate, as any account does.
#   - the KEY: Linux keeps it in plain text in a file only sdai can read (0600).  Here it is a DPAPI blob made in the service account's own user scope (owner, Q4),
#     in a file whose access list holds only that account and SYSTEM; a key file that grants anyone else is refused, not repaired, as Linux refuses a 0640 one.
#     ON WINDOWS SDSYS IS AN ELEVATED ADMINISTRATOR, AND AN ADMINISTRATOR CAN OBTAIN THE KEY (owner, Q5): Linux's sdsys cannot.  No tighter form exists.
#   - the pipe: FILE_FLAG_FIRST_PIPE_INSTANCE (a pipe of that name made by anyone else first stops the service, loudly), PIPE_REJECT_REMOTE_CLIENTS (a pipe is
#     reachable over the network otherwise; a Unix socket never is), its owner this account (the kernel checks it before sending), and a protected access list:
#     SYSTEM and this account full, sdusers and Administrators PIPE_CLIENT_MASK only (read, write, attributes, the owner: no FILE_CREATE_PIPE_INSTANCE).
#   - the watchdog: Linux's alarm(); here a timer in the process, and a job object that ends every request when the service stops.
#   - locks: Linux's flock(); here a byte lock (msvcrt.locking).  ca-file: a drive path written with forward slashes (C:/...), as SET.AI can carry it.
#
# START-HISTORY:
# 13 Nov 26 Windows port - from Linux b1d496df (RELEASE_1.1 246).  The decisions, the order, the providers, the wire formats, the limits, hygiene, the settings
#               and their checks are Linux's; the Windows facts above replace peer_uid, in_group, is_admin, read_key/write_key, counters' flock, the alarm, and
#               systemd's socket (the --service and --listen loop).
# 8 Nov 26 dm   S.170 (the owner, after "Deepseek-V4-Pro" came back as a bare HTTP 400: "is there any way to verify the model name entry, or provide a dropdown list of valid models"):
#               op models, the administrator's, asks the provider for its list of models (GET .../models with the same key and certificate rules; nothing of anyone's text is
#               sent); a refusal now carries the provider's own words (cut, ASCII, the key replaced; none for 401 / 403); https_post became https_call, and the network
#               refusals and the status rules are call_once and check_status, shared by ask and the list.
# 7 Nov 26 dm   S.163 (the owner: "it is impossible to give ai access to sdsys, it should be available to sdsys"): may_ask() - the administrator (sdsys, root) may
#               ask without the sdaiusers gate, which sdsys could never be given (modify.account refuses the name); status's "gate" says the same.
# 7 Nov 26 dm   S.160, the standing text (the owner: "a standard text that will be included with every AI query ... editable but supplied with the default text"):
#               set what=context keeps the administrator's text in the state directory, status hands it (or the shipped default) to a gate member and the administrator.
# 3 Nov 26 dm   Follow-up questions (the owner: "Add, shown in preview"): an ask may carry "history", a list of at most 3 earlier exchanges {"q": question, "a": answer}, which SD
#               sends ONLY when the person chose Follow up (and showed in the preview).  They go to the provider as earlier user / assistant messages before the request, in
#               both wire formats (Custom speaks one of the two, so it takes them the same way); the bytes-request limit counts them.  A history of another shape is badreq.
# 1 Nov 26 dm   Google is a shipped provider (its OpenAI-compatible endpoint), and every shipped provider has a STARTING model (model= in the providers file): the model asked for is
#               the administrator's (set model) or, while there is none, that one; a status for sdsys and root carries both ("model" the set one, "default" the starting one), and
#               the status's own "model" is the one that will be asked for.  Custom has no starting model.  The owner: "pre-filled in except for the key ... an option for a user
#               specified model".
# 23 Oct 26 dm  The settings (set, keyset, keyclear) are made here, by sdsys and root, instead of by sd-elevate verbs as the plan first said: the file and the keys are
#               sdai's own, the one check is the peer's uid, and the same validators serve both the reading and the writing.
# 23 Oct 26 dm  New.  Group A of plan-ai-step2: non-streaming (the framing is line-based so a streamed reply can follow), two wire formats
#               (anthropic, openai), the three shipped providers and the owner's fourth, Custom.
# END-HISTORY

